PaleJS
@rtepl/pale is the JavaScript/TypeScript SDK for Node.js. Use it to compile workspace configuration, validate a contract document, or explain a proposed change. It calls the canonical Rust SDK, pale-sdk. api.capabilityIds lists the operations available in this host. Other SDKs and interfaces can support a different set of operations; shared operations keep the same contract identities, meaning, diagnostics, and effect classifications.
Build the local addon
Build this package with Node.js 22 or later, Rust 1.94 or later, and the npm dependencies pinned by package-lock.json. From sdk/js in this checkout, with those dependencies available, run:
npm run build

The build creates the native addon, its index.js loader and TypeScript declarations. The JavaScript API reference describes the public types and operations. Cargo may download dependencies. The examples import ./index.js from this build; there is no registry installation step or resident startup.
Compile a workspace from memory
Use compilePortableWorkspace when your application already has the source buffers, for example in an editor. Supply a root label and a complete array of { label, bytes } documents. Each label is relative to the logical workspace; each bytes value is a Node Buffer. Include the root manifest and the module and lock documents it selects.
After building the addon, save this as compile-example.mjs in sdk/js and run node compile-example.mjs. It loads the complete workspace example:
import bindings from "./index.js";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";

const folder = new URL("../../docs/examples/workspace/", import.meta.url);
const documents = [
  "pale-workspace.toml",
  "resolution.lock.toml",
  "universe.lock.toml",
].map((label) => ({ label, bytes: readFileSync(new URL(label, folder)) }));
const api = new bindings.Pale();
const workspace = api.compilePortableWorkspace({
  rootManifest: "pale-workspace.toml",
  documents,
});
console.log(
  `${workspace.workspaceId}: ${workspace.sourceDocumentCount} documents`,
);

Expect notes-workspace: 3 documents. The returned compilation supports authoring queries and comparison with a separately compiled candidate. In an editor, replace the loader with its buffers. A missing document or invalid workspace produces the API diagnostic rather than a smaller successful result.
For the declared project, execution-setting and machine selections, use the workspace contract. For ordinary command-line use, follow Inspect your workspace.
Inspect a contract
A contract schema describes the fields a document can contain. This example looks up the schema for a Pale diagnostic, then handles an unavailable schema. It reads the addon's embedded data; it does not write files or start a resident service.
After building the addon, save this JavaScript as example.mjs in this directory, then run node example.mjs:
import bindings from "./index.js";

const api = new bindings.Pale();
const schema = api.contractSchema("diagnostic.v1");
console.log(
  JSON.stringify(
    {
      contract: schema.registryId,
      schemaSha256: schema.schemaSha256,
    },
    null,
    2,
  ),
);

try {
  api.contractSchema("not-a-contract.v1");
} catch (error) {
  if (!(error instanceof Error)) throw error;
  console.error(error.diagnostic?.summary ?? error.message);
}

The first call returns the schema identity and a SHA-256 digest of its exact bytes. The second reports that the requested contract is unavailable. API failures provide structured details in error.diagnostic; use its fields rather than parsing the message. Initialization or host errors can instead be ordinary Error values, which is why the example keeps a message fallback.
To check a document, pass its Buffer to api.validateContractDocument(schemaId, bytes). Successful structural validation checks the document's shape, not its authority to run an operation. The API reference describes the requests and results for this and the other available operations.
Compile and review safely
Compilation returns an opaque handle: a native object that your program passes back to the API instead of recreating its internal state. Generated TypeScript types reject plain objects and handles of the wrong class. A type assertion can bypass that check, so the native binding also checks class identity at runtime.
await api.compileOneShot(request) copies the captured input before returning its promise. A Node-API worker validates the input and renders the outputs; the promise returns a compilation handle or rejects with the API diagnostic. await api.previewLocalConfigurationImprovement(request) also runs on the worker pool. It captures two absolute workspace manifests under one common project, execution workspace and machine, then returns the existing review preview. Only the baseline supplies Package source. Capture is not an atomic filesystem snapshot or a view of unsaved editor buffers. Other operations are synchronous and can block an editor's event loop. This worker route adds neither an abort operation nor permission to cause native effects.
Configuration review advances through API stages. Advancing a consuming stage uses up its handle; your program cannot reuse that stage afterward. The separate prepare/commit route lets you retry failed preparation with the same explanation. Commit requires that exact explanation object, not another with an equal digest. Preview queries reuse the saved comparison and profile handles. Accept, reject, cancel and stale describe review outcomes: none writes configuration or grants permission to cause a native effect.
api.compiledInterfaceIdentity() reports compiled SDK version, interface and target claims. api.verifyCurrentExecutableBinding(compilation) checks the current Node executable against the captured workspace binding. It does not check the loaded addon's bytes. Neither call observes a resident or establishes process health. Local settlement checks this same binding before making changes. A workspace bound to a separate Pale CLI executable cannot be settled directly from Node.
Inspect organizations
Use this branch when your application needs to browse declared organizations and relationships. For compilation or candidate review, continue with Compile and review safely instead. The generated API reference defines each request, returned record and continuation type; search for inspectOrganization to find its complete signature.
Read endpoints and records
Use inspectOrganizationEndpoint to find an exact typed endpoint and inspectOrganizationIncidentRelations to read its incoming and outgoing relations. Both query the retained compilation. A found record does not prove effective state or native readiness; an empty page with a continuation does not prove absence.
Use PortableWorkspaceCompilation.inspectOrganization to browse organization and responsibility records, then use each returned handle to read its detailed fields and source mapping. Communication, service, projection and genealogy members can be read independently. Every record variant carries its typed payload; u64 intervals and retention use bigint, not lossy JavaScript numbers. Source mappings distinguish unmapped records from named empty, incomplete and terminal pages.
Continue a query
Record limits are explicit integers from 1 through 256; copy budgets are integers from 1 through the SDK-owned 4 MiB ceiling. No zero default or 257 clamp applies. Changing the owner, record, nested parent, section or original limits rejects a continuation. Errors preserve SDK diagnostics; rejected queries publish no partial page and do not consume the original cursor. Limits count SDK query visits and copied UTF-8 payload, not total native/JavaScript memory, repeated getter copies, source text, encoded bytes or retained compilation size. All handles are factory-only; copied values and digests cannot recreate them. Mutating a JavaScript copy never changes the retained SDK observation.
This is inspection of caller-supplied admitted inputs, not recipient disclosure, resident authentication or native effect. It adds no geometry or transport schema. PaleJS also exposes opaque recipient-qualified inspection result types for trusted application hosts. JavaScript cannot construct them, and the current resident attachment does not yet return them. Do not substitute a portable compilation or public digest for that missing authority-bearing acquisition step.
Find the same record across a change
WorkspaceComparison.correspondOrganizationRecord searches the other retained compilation using the selected record's canonical key. Select Prior or Current explicitly; a searching cursor is not absence. A match returns the opposite-side entry and the SDK's complete value comparison, including nested fields omitted from shallow rows. sameRecord identifies the exact retained owner and record, including independently paged handles, but never equates equal-valued generations. The original pair, selected handle, direction and raw limits bind continuation; copy-budget failure leaves that cursor usable. Neither query authorizes disclosure.
Verify the package
The package manifest owns the napi-rs build-tool pin. The contracts directory owns the generated Apache-licensed @rtepl/pale-contracts TypeScript package; neither package duplicates Rust compiler, admission, diagnostic or graph policy.
Run npm test to rebuild and exercise all eligible operations through the real addon using the shared API test cases. It also checks deterministic declaration generation and installs the packed addon into an isolated temporary consumer. That consumer checks strict TypeScript, calls the addon, rejects forged handles, and verifies that removing the declared Node types breaks type checking. These checks use cached npm dependencies offline; they do not publish the package or install it into a user's workspace. Cargo tests alone do not test this complete JavaScript-to-native call boundary.
The organization checks exercise generated declarations, the native ABI and the same inspection behavior through the installed packed package. Their fixtures are exported from the application and checked against its source bytes; the fixture README describes regeneration. These tests exercise supplied-input inspection and do not grant recipient access to a resident.
The declaration build uses the pinned TypeScript parser to preserve native class identity and hide constructors for factory-created handles. It adds no runtime fields. Plain request/result records remain structural types, and byte fields use Node's Buffer through the pinned @types/node dependency.

Pale SDK for Rust
Use pale-sdk to compile a workspace in your application, inspect the configuration it describes and compare a proposed change. The same API supplies Pale's language bindings, command line and editor interfaces. Your application supplies the inputs; Pale returns validated results and handles for further queries.
Use Rust 1.94 or later and build from the Pale checkout. Its .cargo/config.toml resolves the matching local 0.0.1 dependencies; these packages are not a published registry release. The example runs Cargo from that root so you need no extra dependency patches.
Compile workspace documents
An editor can compile the documents it already holds without making the SDK read files. Supply the root manifest's logical relative label and the complete labelled source set it selects, including its module and lock documents. The labels identify those supplied bytes; they are not paths the SDK opens on your machine.
Use the complete workspace example for a first call. Create a fresh application directory outside the checkout, with a src subdirectory. Save this as its Cargo.toml, replacing the dependency path with the absolute path to this checkout's sdk/rust directory:
[package]
name = "pale-workspace-example"
version = "0.0.0"
edition = "2024"

[dependencies]
pale-sdk = { path = "/absolute/path/to/pale/sdk/rust" }

Save the following program as src/main.rs in that application directory:
use pale_sdk::{PaleApi, PortableWorkspaceDocument, PortableWorkspaceRequest};

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let documents = ["pale-workspace.toml", "resolution.lock.toml", "universe.lock.toml"]
        .into_iter()
        .map(|label| {
            let bytes = std::fs::read(format!("docs/examples/workspace/{label}"))?;
            Ok(PortableWorkspaceDocument::new(label.to_owned(), bytes))
        })
        .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, std::io::Error>>()?;
    let api = PaleApi::new()?;
    let workspace = api.compile_portable_workspace(
        PortableWorkspaceRequest::new("pale-workspace.toml".to_owned(), documents),
    )?;

    println!("{}: {} documents", workspace.workspace_id(), workspace.source_document_count());
    for context in workspace.profile_contexts() {
        println!("{} / {}", context.project_id(), context.execution_workspace_id());
    }
    Ok(())
}

From the Pale repository root, run the application, replacing the manifest path with the one you just saved:
cargo run --manifest-path /absolute/path/to/pale-workspace-example/Cargo.toml

Keep that working directory: Cargo reads the checkout's .cargo/config.toml to resolve the SDK's matching internal dependencies, and the program reads docs/examples/workspace/ relative to the same root. The first run may download Rust dependencies and creates a lockfile in your example application. No edit to Pale's Cargo manifests is needed.
Expect notes-workspace: 3 documents followed by notes / local. This program's loader reads the files; the SDK receives only labels and bytes. Replace the loader with your editor's buffers when embedding it. A missing document or invalid relationship returns an ApiError instead of a partly compiled world.
The returned handle keeps the validated workspace available. Its summary() provides displayable counts and identities; pass the handle itself, not a copied summary, to operations that need the compilation. To compare a proposed edit, compile the candidate documents separately and call compare_portable_workspaces with both handles. Comparison and impact queries retain their own paging limits.
Portable workspace compilation describes the declared world. Package guidance also needs the selected Package inputs and effective-profile compilation; runtime settings and operation contracts need complete one-shot compilation. The workspace contract explains the project, execution setting and machine selections used for that work. For command-line use rather than embedding, continue with Inspect your workspace.
Generate Package guidance
Compile the portable workspace, then call compile_effective_profile with its project/execution selectors, complete private inputs and exact Package documents. The returned EffectiveProfileCompilation can render source guidance for an explicit repository and host:
use pale_sdk::{EffectiveProfileCompilation, SourceGuidanceHost, SourceGuidanceProjection};

fn inspect_guidance(
    profile: &EffectiveProfileCompilation,
    repository_id: &str,
) -> Result<SourceGuidanceProjection, pale_sdk::ApiError> {
    let guidance = profile.source_guidance(repository_id, SourceGuidanceHost::Codex)?;
    for file in guidance.files() {
        println!("{} {}", file.sha256(), file.rel_path());
    }
    Ok(guidance)
}

ClaudeCode selects Claude's native skills and references. Both hosts return AGENTS.md from entrypoint_path(). The result contains proposed skills, role references and a reading index. entrypoint_contribution() is a small proposed addition to that shared file, separate from complete files; it does not take ownership of existing project instructions. Required doctrine and inherited role lenses remain composed once, and optional subjects stay linked. Local project facts and native tool bindings retain their owners. Claude's native loading setting is a separate host concern.
This is a source-only Rust API. It observes no host or checkout and writes no files. An adopter must separately review the exact destinations, preserve project instructions and qualify fresh file baselines before writing. The result cannot be used as a runtime saved plan. Selections containing operations, recipes, modes or lifecycle delivery/gates are refused rather than silently dropped; use the admitted runtime path for those duties. Source guidance does not make the ordinary CLI a resident client or grant native tool invocation.
With native-local, compile_local_effective_profile accepts the existing LocalOneShotCompilationRequest selectors and captures the required local source files through the bounded capture owner. It returns the same retained profile, stopping before runtime rendering. This adds filesystem reads, without observing a host's readiness or writing guidance to a repository.
EffectiveProfileCompilation::repository_context(repository_id) returns private context separately from source_guidance: selected scope, repository access and role, and the session's ordered reading references with their configured machine authority roots. Every reading root requires declared read-only access. Each binding and relative reference remains separate: determining whether the root is a directory or an exact file requires native observation. The method neither reads the referenced documents nor writes repository files, and its machine paths must stay within the project's disclosure scope. Missing reading order is reported explicitly. This renders existing session declarations; it does not infer ownership, accept duties or admit a host. The CLI exposes it as guidance context with the same workspace selectors.
For a local reconciliation proposal, call plan_local_source_guidance with LocalSourceGuidancePlanRequest::new(selection, repository_id, host, intent). Here selection is the same LocalOneShotCompilationRequest, host is a SourceGuidanceHost, and intent is LocalSourceGuidanceIntent::Apply or Remove. The SDK captures the exact selected canonical checkout, private ownership record and required file/parent baselines through the filesystem adapter, observes those paths again and delegates reconciliation to the application planner. Missing runtime paths do not prevent source planning; runtime-dependent selections still prevent an apply proposal. Removal can plan retirement of existing source guidance after a runtime operation is selected.
LocalSourceGuidancePlan::report() returns the SDK-owned serializable inventory, baseline hashes/modes, observed UTF-8 text, exact proposed bytes, publication order and plan digest. Before/after entry text includes local facts: serialization is an explicit local review operation, not a public telemetry payload. The opaque handle's debug output omits target paths and bodies. Root and file identities stay private. The application's budget covers observed plus proposed payload bytes, not process memory or extra caller-owned report copies.
Planning writes nothing and creates no lock or ownership state. Its digest is content/baseline identity, not effect authority or a runtime saved plan. Pass the same selection and reviewed digest to apply_local_source_guidance(LocalSourceGuidanceWriteRequest::new(plan_request, digest)). The SDK rebuilds sources and native baselines, obtains application admission for that exact review, and invokes the confined filesystem publisher. Its owned result reports the confirmed plan, changed-file count and separate coordination effect. A pending recovery journal blocks normal writes. Publication failure distinguishes not-started, restored before-images and uncertain outcomes; uncertainty requires native inspection before retry.
For a user-facing plan/apply flow, display the report, then call remember_local_source_guidance_plan(&plan). This separate save writes only the proposal identity in a mode-0600 root file for its host; it does not establish user approval. A later LocalSourceGuidanceWriteRequest::remembered(plan_request) retrieves that identity and uses the same fresh-source and target checks. A new plan replaces that host's remembered review. After an apply changes files, review a new plan before another apply. The CLI follows this flow, including when its report is JSON.
For a pending journal, construct LocalSourceGuidanceRecoveryRequest from the same selection, repository and host, with an explicit LocalSourceGuidanceRecoveryIntent::RestoreBefore or FinalizePublished. plan_local_source_guidance_recovery validates logical ownership before exposing an optional local review. None means no pending journal was observed, not host readiness. Its report gives exact proposed bytes, evidence and conditional directory cleanup; serialization may disclose local before-images. execute_local_source_guidance_recovery takes LocalSourceGuidanceRecoveryExecutionRequest::new(recovery_request, digest) and rebuilds the observations before exact reviewed admission. Restore preserves independent edits; finalize requires a complete after-state and fills no missing work. An uncheckpointed matching after-image remains uncertain execution origin.
The filesystem adapter owns publication, before-image custody and recovery. The application plan includes a .gitignore block for the persistent root lock, private pending journal and remembered reviews, preserving other rules; its contents appear in the same review and transaction as the guidance. The block remains after removal while the lock and review files remain. Git's administrative directories are untouched, including in linked worktrees. See the CLI guide for exact paths and terminal use. These APIs are available through Rust and the CLI. Actual coding-host use remains separate; no MCP, language-binding operation or runtime invocation grant is implied.
Compare an edited workspace
In the program above, insert this before Ok(()). It keeps the original compilation, changes the project's title in a second source batch and reads every bounded page of impacts:
let candidate_documents = ["pale-workspace.toml", "resolution.lock.toml", "universe.lock.toml"]
    .into_iter()
    .map(|label| {
        let text = std::fs::read_to_string(format!("docs/examples/workspace/{label}"))?;
        let text = if label == "pale-workspace.toml" {
            text.replace("Shared notes", "Team notes")
        } else { text };
        Ok(PortableWorkspaceDocument::new(label.to_owned(), text.into_bytes()))
    })
    .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, std::io::Error>>()?;
let candidate = api.compile_portable_workspace(PortableWorkspaceRequest::new(
    "pale-workspace.toml".to_owned(), candidate_documents,
))?;
let comparison = api.compare_portable_workspaces(&workspace, &candidate)?;
println!("{} source changes", comparison.change_root_count());
let mut offset = 0;
loop {
    let page = comparison.impacts(offset, 10);
    for impact in page.entries() {
        let values = comparison.compare_subject(impact.subject());
        if let pale_sdk::PortableWorkspaceSubjectValues::Shared { comparison: value, .. } = values.values() {
            println!("{:?}: {:?}", impact.state(), value);
        }
    }
    match page.next_offset() {
        Some(next) => offset = next,
        None => break,
    }
}

The comparison reports one source change without changing either file on disk. Its rows include Affected: Equal: an input changed, but the selected subject's compiled value did not. Use compare_subject for a subject's prior/current values and explain to trace why it is affected. Impact means a dependency on the changed input; it does not by itself mean that the subject's resulting value differs. The local method reference defines these methods on PortableWorkspaceComparison, including paging and retained-handle rules.
Read contract data and handle errors
PaleApi::new() verifies the contracts embedded in the binary. You can retrieve a schema to inspect its fields or validate a document against its selected contract:
use pale_sdk::PaleApi;

fn print_diagnostic_schema() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let api = PaleApi::new()?;
    let schema = api.contract_schema("diagnostic.v1")?;
    println!("{}: {} bytes", schema.schema_uri(), schema.bytes().len());
    Ok(())
}

API failures carry structured diagnostics; use error.diagnostic() for their fields rather than parsing the displayed message. API initialization can fail separately. A protocol adapter that needs only diagnostic conversion can use DiagnosticProjector without initializing the unrelated operation catalog.
Choose portable, native or resident operations
Portable methods use supplied data. The optional native-local feature adds filesystem and process operations; each call still has its own inputs and effect permissions. Each language binding exposes its supported subset.
A resident is Pale's long-running local service. Resident operation methods require a caller-supplied ResidentAttachment with an authenticated carrier and response checks. Start returns a durable handle before dispatch; advance performs one bounded turn; Observe reads signed records; Cancel requests cancellation whose result must still be observed. The SDK does not discover or start that service.
Hook input admission and captured native observations are separate, effect-free steps. admit_resident_hook checks a static route and bounded native event; it supplies no active session or carrier. For an admitted original pre-tool event, observe_codex_command_completion(&admission, started_bytes, completed_bytes) checks captured Codex v2 command notifications. The codec checks the exact session, turn, tool-use identity, reported cwd, native command representation and lifecycle phases. It preserves terminal item status and an optional typed process exit independently. Missing exit remains missing, regardless of output text. The result retains identities rather than raw IDs, commands or output.
deliver_resident_hook consumes an admitted event and sends its exact native bytes and static route through a hook-only or operation attachment. Use ResidentAttachment::provision_hooks for blocking hook delivery without signed-record admission, operation commands or Observe access. With native-local on macOS and Linux, load_hook_configuration loads the explicitly selected protected configuration described in the CLI guide. Observation-only attachments cannot send hooks. The complete frame is limited to 256 KiB, including its JSON envelope. Delivery uses the attachment's shared queue and deadline and never retries automatically.
An embedder with an independent response authority can use ResidentAttachment::provision_admitted_hooks. deliver_resident_hook then returns Result<ResidentHookOutput, ApiError>: the exact native standard output, standard error and exit status become available only after the signed dispatch records and response pass that authority's checks. The resident prepares the selected permission and context response, saves its preparation and dispatch with their checkpoints and independent anchor, then checks current request, session, permission and disclosure authority again before release. The caller verifies the native request and response digests and requires independent signature, authority and one-use progress admission; ordinary operation-observation admission cannot authorize a hook response.
Important

Select and qualify response authority before permitting native work. The protected file configuration and provision_hooks do not supply it, so they continue to block native work, including reads. Production response authority and deployment integration for interrupted-response recovery remain unfinished. A stopped response append, replay conflict, lost reply or malformed or late response requires reconciliation before another attempt; connecting successfully does not establish enforcement by the host.
This Rust-only codec was qualified against Codex 0.144.1 observations. The integration must independently qualify its actual binary/configuration and authenticate the carrier. The original hook command and native shell-wrapped command have separate identities; best-effort commandActions establishes no equivalence. Reading captured notifications neither registers an operation nor consumes an invocation. The first repository-verification duty still requires the admitted durable-operation mapping and one-shot consumption before its native result can settle that duty. No CLI, MCP or language-binding exposure is advertised for this observation helper.
The attachment does not resend a failed request. A separate later call can request a fresh connection. Returned signed records keep their exact canonical JSON, and Rust callers can borrow the original admitted bytes. Effect-free A2A handle inspection checks only the handle's structure, digest and operation class; live custody and signature checks need the resident attachment.
Build the method reference
From this directory:
cargo doc --no-deps --features native-local --open

Cargo opens Rustdoc from its active target directory, normally target/doc/pale_sdk/index.html in this checkout. This is a local reference; the documentation site does not currently publish the complete Rust API. Open PaleApi for compilation, PortableWorkspaceComparison for source changes and impacts, and ResidentAttachment for the carrier and response-checking contracts.
The method reference contains the request types, handle transitions and detailed failure conditions for the operations above. Building it does not run a resident operation.

Pale TypeScript contracts
Use @rtepl/pale-contracts for TypeScript declarations, standalone validators, and builders for eligible authored inputs. It is generated from Pale's initial-v1 JSON Schema bundle. Use it to check JSON values without loading the native engine. Rust records and the contract registry define those shapes.
Validate a document
Build this local private package from sdk/js/contracts with Node.js 22 or later and its pinned npm dependencies available:
npm run build

Save this as validate-example.mjs in that directory. The sample is a structurally valid diagnostic with this build's schema identity; it does not claim that a real operation failed:
import { CONTRACT_IDENTITIES, validate } from "./dist/index.js";

const diagnostic = {
  schema: "pale/diagnostic.v1",
  schema_sha256: CONTRACT_IDENTITIES["diagnostic.v1"],
  schema_version: 1,
  code: "example_missing_project",
  stage: "input_admission",
  severity: "error",
  disposition: "invalid_input",
  summary: "Choose a project before compiling its profile.",
  retry: "after_correction",
  effect: "not_applicable",
  truncation: {
    shortened_fields: 0, omitted_causes: 0, omitted_notes: 0,
    omitted_help: 0, omitted_facts: 0,
  },
};
const result = validate("diagnostic.v1", diagnostic);
if (result.valid) {
  console.log(`${result.value.code}: ${result.value.summary}`);
} else {
  console.error(result.errors);
  process.exitCode = 1;
}

Run node validate-example.mjs. Expect example_missing_project: Choose a project before compiling its profile. Replace the sample value with parsed JSON from your application when checking a real response. Invalid documents report schema errors. Import by package name in a consumer where this local package is installed; this is not a published-registry install.
Type declarations describe values at compile time; validate checks unknown runtime values. Neither establishes an operation's semantic admission or its permission to run. For workspace compilation or API calls, use PaleJS or PaleWasm.
Build an authored candidate
AUTHORED_INPUT_FAMILIES lists the schema families accepted by buildAuthoredInput(family, value). The builder validates, copies and freezes the JSON tree, then returns its canonical JSON alongside the typed value. It rejects a family that is not an authored input. This is useful when your application must preserve exactly what it will submit; it performs no I/O, signing or execution. Submit the candidate through the responsible Pale API for its semantic checks.
For example, prepare a capability declaration that selects no callable tools:
import { buildAuthoredInput } from "./dist/index.js";

const source = { capability_bindings: {} };
const candidate = buildAuthoredInput("capabilities.v1", source);
console.log(candidate.canonicalJson);
console.log(Object.isFrozen(candidate.value));

This prints {"capability_bindings":{}} and true. It is a usable empty capability document to extend, not an installed provider or a runnable operation. Changing source later leaves the retained candidate unchanged. A consumer that needs an actual tool must supply its real provider binding before passing that declaration to Pale for semantic checks.
Keep the generated contracts current
The generator writes declarations, Ajv 2020 validators, builder eligibility, and manifest identities into src/generated. Its source coordinate records the exact registry, generator, dependency lock, behavior options, and ordered output set. This lets the package check freshness from exact inputs instead of guessing from a version number.
Run npm test to compile the checked-in projection, replay the JCS vectors, and exercise validation and builder confinement. Source freshness is a separate composition-root check because this independent package does not assume a parent checkout:
npm run check:fresh -- --bundle <canonical-agent-surface.v1-directory>

PaleWasm
@rtepl/pale-wasm is the WebAssembly SDK. Use it from JavaScript or TypeScript to inspect contracts, validate documents, and compile supplied workspace sources. It runs the portable operations of the canonical Rust SDK in WebAssembly.
This is the portable, effect-free subset of pale-sdk, not the full native API. It cannot read repository files, start processes, contact the resident or providers, or install, activate, or apply changes. Callers supply source bytes; successful compilation does not prove that a machine or provider is ready.
Build the local package
Use Rust 1.94 or later, wasm-pack, and Node.js 22 or later. From sdk/wasm in this checkout, run:
npm run build

The build produces dist/ in wasm-bindgen's bundler format. It may download Rust or WebAssembly build dependencies. A consumer also needs the matching local @rtepl/pale-contracts package. The Contracts and package checks section below describes its ownership and freshness checks. These private 0.0.1 packages are not a promise of registry availability. Building them does not activate Pale.
Prepare a local consumer
After building both packages, pack them into an existing output directory. Run each npm pack from its own package directory and retain the filenames it prints:
npm pack --ignore-scripts --pack-destination /absolute/path/artifacts

In a new empty consumer directory, create package.json with {"private":true,"type":"module"}. Install the two exact local archives:
npm install --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund \
  /absolute/path/artifacts/rtepl-pale-contracts-0.0.1.tgz \
  /absolute/path/artifacts/rtepl-pale-wasm-0.0.1.tgz

Both archives must come from the same contract bundle. Installing both explicitly avoids asking a registry for this private dependency. Use the Node-hosted example below to check that consumer; a browser application needs a bundler that supports WebAssembly-module imports and its own integration checks.
First use
Use an ESM consumer with the locally built @rtepl/pale-wasm package and its matching @rtepl/pale-contracts dependency available. The host must support the bundler distribution's WebAssembly-module imports. It does not expose a separate init() step.
Save this as example.mjs in that consumer. It prints the selected diagnostic schema's identity, then demonstrates a rejected schema lookup:
import { Pale } from "@rtepl/pale-wasm";

const api = new Pale();
try {
  const schema = api.contractSchema("diagnostic.v1");
  try {
    console.log(
      JSON.stringify(
        {
          schema: schema.registryId,
          sha256: schema.schemaSha256,
        },
        null,
        2,
      ),
    );
  } finally {
    schema.free();
  }

  try {
    api.contractSchema("not-a-selected-schema");
  } catch (error) {
    if (!(error instanceof Error) || !error.diagnostic) throw error;
    console.error(
      JSON.stringify(
        {
          code: error.diagnostic.code,
          message: error.message,
        },
        null,
        2,
      ),
    );
  }
} finally {
  api.free();
}

For the Node-hosted check, use Node 22 or later:
node --experimental-wasm-modules example.mjs

Expect schema: "diagnostic.v1" and its build-specific hash on standard output, then error code contract_rejected on standard error. This is an intentional input failure; correct the requested registry identity rather than retrying it unchanged. Ordinary API failures carry diagnostic and exact canonical JSON in diagnosticJson. Do not assume every thrown value has those properties: initialization can throw a string, and host/WebAssembly failures are separate. If the host cannot construct an error or attach diagnostic data, that host failure is returned instead of an incomplete Pale diagnostic.
A rejected workspace can also supply authoringCorrections and an authoringLocation. Corrections contain the exact expected text, replacement, source path, and compiler evidence for a proposed edit. The array is empty when no correction is proved; the location is undefined when unavailable. These values do not change diagnostic bytes or apply an edit. Check each value's native class before using it, and release its WebAssembly handle when finished.
Methods run synchronously. Release returned WebAssembly handles with free() when finished, and do not reuse handles after freeing or consuming them. The generated API reference describes method arguments, results, and available getters. For example, compilePortableWorkspace consumes its request; do not reuse or free that request afterward. api.capabilityIdsJson() lists the operations selected for this build; absence is not permission to recreate them in the host.
For a browser or extension, verify the example through your actual bundler and run compilation in a worker so it does not block the UI. A desktop editor already using the native LSP does not need a second WASM compiler for the same work.
Compile a workspace from memory
Supply the root manifest's logical relative label and the complete source set from your editor or loader. Each document has a label and Uint8Array bytes; include the root and the module and lock documents it selects.
With the local package available in the ESM consumer used above, save this as compile-example.mjs. It reads the complete workspace example from a source checkout path supplied on the command line:
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { resolve } from "node:path";
import { pathToFileURL } from "node:url";
import {
  Pale,
  PortableWorkspaceDocument,
  PortableWorkspaceRequest,
} from "@rtepl/pale-wasm";

if (!process.argv[2]) throw new Error("Pass the Pale source checkout path");
const folder = pathToFileURL(
  resolve(process.argv[2], "docs/examples/workspace") + "/",
);
const documents = [
  "pale-workspace.toml",
  "resolution.lock.toml",
  "universe.lock.toml",
].map(
  (label) =>
    new PortableWorkspaceDocument(label, readFileSync(new URL(label, folder))),
);
const api = new Pale();
try {
  const request = new PortableWorkspaceRequest(
    "pale-workspace.toml",
    documents,
  );
  const workspace = api.compilePortableWorkspace(request);
  try {
    console.log(
      `${workspace.workspaceId}: ${workspace.sourceDocumentCount} documents`,
    );
  } finally {
    workspace.free();
  }
} finally {
  api.free();
}

Run node --experimental-wasm-modules compile-example.mjs /absolute/path/to/pale. Expect notes-workspace: 3 documents. The request consumes its documents and compilation consumes the request; do not free or reuse those consumed wrappers. The example releases the returned compilation after reading it. Keep it alive longer if you need authoring queries or a comparison with a second compilation. For browser use, replace the Node loader with fetched files or editor buffers.
Portable compilation does not load machine bindings, Package implementations or a resident. The workspace contract explains the complete selection; Inspect your workspace shows the command-line workflow for an existing configured workspace.
Contracts and package checks
The JavaScript entry point consumes @rtepl/pale-contracts/registry and re-exports SCHEMA_REGISTRY_ID, SCHEMA_MANIFEST_SHA256, and SCHEMA_SET_SHA256. api.contractBundleIdentity() reports the bundle embedded in WASM; the package consumer test checks that its manifest and set match the contract package. Neither entry point generates another contract corpus. Import @rtepl/pale-contracts directly when you need its standalone schemas, validators, or pure builders; the WASM entry point does not load that validator bundle.
Cargo tests check Rust-side translation laws; npm test also exercises the built WebAssembly boundary and the shared conformance fixtures.
The package-consumer gate needs an explicitly selected packed @rtepl/pale-contracts artifact and an installed TypeScript compiler:
npm test -- --contracts /absolute/path/rtepl-pale-contracts-0.0.1.tgz \
  --typescript /absolute/path/typescript/bin/tsc

npm run test:package -- accepts the same arguments for the focused gate. It builds the bundler distribution, packs this package, and installs only those two local artifacts into an isolated temporary consumer, offline and without install scripts. Strict TypeScript compilation and an actual package-name import check the canonical diagnostic and embedded/contract-package identity join, including rejection of a mismatched set coordinate.

Diagnostics
In Scope's design the diagnostic stream lets a separately owned interface show what the service is doing; nothing runs yet, because the kernel is an empty library. Scope itself stays headless. One authenticated viewer at a time receives only the state it may see, filtered before serialisation: resolves, reported context use, score projections, omissions, imports, rebuilds, errors and health. Owner-outcome events additionally depend on the deferred execution identity contract described below.
The stream is a rebuildable local projection. Durable records stay in Scope's store; ecosystem events enter its inbox by reference, and Pale observes Scope through its own event contract. An interface may render the stream inside Reckon's debugger; the backend and the isolation tests it needs are the work in front.
The daemon exposes one stream beside its operations
Scope daemon (one process)
  ├── deployment-selected local transport      native callers; authenticated
  ├── Dolt on 3309                             separate process
  └── restricted loopback HTTP and WebSocket    optional, for an admitted browser plugin only
        ├── resolve, bundle read, use report, health, operator reload
        └── /ws, the diagnostic stream

Viewers present Pale's short-lived admission reference when integrated, or the exact standalone grant and signed request admission; Scope never mints a principal, a credential or a general capability. Host and Origin validation and loopback binding are defence in depth, never admission. Every endpoint shares one daemon state: the graph projection, the persisted episodes, the snapshot counter and the rebuildable telemetry projection. The protocol is a snapshot on connect followed by deltas, with an epoch and sequence cursor for resume, an explicit schema version and disconnect-with-resume, and it must be built and contract-tested before any consumer relies on it.
Every frame carries the same envelope
interface ScopeDiagnosticEventV1 {
  type: string; // discriminator
  event_id: string; // unique within this daemon epoch
  epoch: string; // daemon boot epoch, new on every start; required for resume
  timestamp: string; // display only
  sequence: number; // monotonic; the ordering key and replay cursor
  schema_version: number; // bumped on any breaking change
}

The epoch and sequence together are the replay cursor, valid only within one epoch. Filtering happens before serialisation: out-of-scope nodes, edges, roles and sources can never influence visible counts, aggregate scores, error shapes, replay gaps or health fields.
The events
Snapshot. Sent once after authentication: the caller-visible nodes, edges, daemon health and consumer activity, never the controller's unfiltered graph. Each node carries its key, type (legal entity, person, site-author projection, brand, project, workspace, domain, email, resource, repository, article, collection, journey), its adapter and source-provenance reference and hash, a label, its content type, and scores for this caller's scope only: the normalised and raw score, maximum, formula version, configuration hash, retrieve and use counts and last retrieved and used times. Each edge carries its key, ends, namespaced relationship type, direction, semantic owner, owner relation reference and hash, source revision, admission, validity, classification, visibility, a display label and an optional derived traversal score. A renderer may show a friendly label but never collapses an owner term such as rights held by, operated by, authored by or collection member into a generic owns, directs, stakeholder or related edge; unknown or unadmitted relation metadata hides the edge rather than guessing.
interface ScopeDiagnosticSnapshotV1 extends ScopeDiagnosticEventV1 {
  type: "scope_diagnostic_snapshot";
  nodes: GraphNode[];
  edges: GraphEdge[];
  daemon: DaemonHealth;
  consumer_activity: ScopeConsumerActivityProjectionV1[];
}

type NodeType =
  | "legal_entity"
  | "person"
  | "website_author_projection"
  | "brand"
  | "project"
  | "workspace"
  | "domain"
  | "email"
  | "resource"
  | "git_repo"
  | "article"
  | "collection"
  | "journey";

type ContentType = "legislative" | "entity" | "transaction" | "ephemeral";

interface GraphNode {
  key: string;
  node_type: NodeType;
  source_adapter_id: string;
  source_provenance_ref: string;
  source_provenance_sha256: string;
  label: string;
  content_type: ContentType;
  score: number;
  raw_score: number;
  raw_max: number;
  formula_version: string;
  config_hash: string;
  retrieve_count: number;
  use_receipt_count: number;
  last_retrieved: string | null;
  last_used: string | null;
  metadata: Record<string, string>;
}

interface GraphEdge {
  edge_key: string;
  source_key: string;
  target_key: string;
  relationship_type_id: string;
  direction: "forward" | "inverse";
  semantic_owner_ref: string;
  owner_relation_ref: string;
  owner_relation_sha256: string;
  source_revision_ref: string;
  admission_ref: string;
  valid_from: string | null;
  valid_until: string | null;
  classification: string;
  visibility: string;
  label: string;
  traversal_score: DerivedScoreV1 | null;
}

interface DerivedScoreV1 {
  value: number;
  formula_version: string;
  evidence_ref: string;
}

The initial stream can project admitted resolve and use records independently. Its schema omits episode_identity, active_episodes and visible_owner_outcome_count, and emits no owner-outcome event. The sketches below include the deferred fields for the execution identity contract. An absent field means that the stream cannot report that measure. Zero is a measured count.
Resolve. After an admitted request returns a bundle: the request and bundle, the caller, the complete execution identity or none, the role, the project, the root of the walk, the nodes and edges traversed, the nodes returned, latency, candidate and returned counts, and the returned nodes' scores at decision time, all caller-visible only.
interface ResolveEvent extends ScopeDiagnosticEventV1 {
  type: "resolve";
  request_id: string;
  bundle_id: string;
  caller_ref: string;
  episode_identity: EpisodeIdentity | null;
  role_ref: string;
  project_key: string | null;
  root_node_key: string;
  traversed_node_keys: string[];
  traversed_edge_keys: [string, string][];
  returned_node_keys: string[];
  latency_ms: number;
  candidate_count: number;
  returned_count: number;
  node_scores: Record<string, number>;
}

Context use. After a use receipt is accepted: the receipt, the bundle and its digest, caller, task, the complete execution identity or none (derived from the signed receipt, never from task text), role, the caller's reported consumed and ignored items, observed staleness and time. The event describes reported use. It conveys no independent model trace, correctness, outcome, credit or score change.
interface ContextUseEvent extends ScopeDiagnosticEventV1 {
  type: "context_use";
  receipt_id: string;
  bundle_id: string;
  bundle_digest: string;
  caller_ref: string;
  task_ref: string | null;
  episode_identity: EpisodeIdentity | null;
  role_ref: string;
  consumed_item_refs: string[];
  ignored_item_refs: string[];
  observed_staleness: string[];
  used_at: string;
}

Owner outcome. After Scope admits an owner's committed terminal event or later outcome evidence: the execution identity, the owner event and digest, a terminal category (succeeded, failed, timed out, cancelled, expired), an error code and duration where known, the outcome evidence references by class (attempt terminal, verifier result, task acceptance, organiser acceptance, owner approval, goal satisfaction) and the episode disposition (closed, duplicate or evidence strengthened). The classes are independent facts, never a ranking.
interface OwnerOutcomeEvent extends ScopeDiagnosticEventV1 {
  type: "owner_outcome";
  episode_identity: EpisodeIdentity;
  owner_event_ref: string;
  owner_event_digest: string;
  terminal_category:
    "succeeded" | "failed" | "timed_out" | "cancelled" | "expired";
  error_code: string | null;
  duration_ms: number | null;
  outcome_evidence_refs: Array<{
    evidence_class:
      | "attempt_terminal"
      | "verifier_result"
      | "task_acceptance"
      | "organiser_acceptance"
      | "owner_approval"
